WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.443
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.743 --> 00:00:09.199
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.499 --> 00:00:18.026
Unit twenty-two: Advanced Determiners. Today you will use either, neither, each, every, whole, all, both and none precisely.

4
00:00:18.326 --> 00:00:20.909
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:25.274
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.117 --> 00:00:26.897
Either.

7
00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:29.860
Either means one or the other of two.

8
00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:31.962
Either road is fine.

9
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.912
Your turn. Say: Either.

10
00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:38.478
Either.

11
00:00:39.217 --> 00:00:40.090
Neither.

12
00:00:41.083 --> 00:00:43.153
Neither means not one and not the other.

13
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.680
Neither answer is correct.

14
00:00:46.283 --> 00:00:48.889
Your turn. Say: Neither.

15
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:52.386
Neither.

16
00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:53.826
Each.

17
00:00:54.817 --> 00:00:57.861
Each means every one, taken individually.

18
00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.950
Each student has a book.

19
00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.773
Your turn. Say: Each.

20
00:01:05.633 --> 00:01:06.483
Each.

21
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:07.972
Every.

22
00:01:08.983 --> 00:01:10.983
Every means all, seen as a group.

23
00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:13.211
Every student has a book.

24
00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:16.376
Your turn. Say: Every.

25
00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:20.083
Every.

26
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:21.549
Whole.

27
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.014
Whole means complete, not divided.

28
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:27.117
The whole class laughed.

29
00:01:27.717 --> 00:01:30.439
Your turn. Say: Whole.

30
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.092
Whole.

31
00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:35.561
All.

32
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.747
All means the total number or amount.

33
00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:40.734
All the children left.

34
00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.793
Your turn. Say: All.

35
00:01:46.633 --> 00:01:47.391
All.

36
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:48.919
Both.

37
00:01:49.917 --> 00:01:51.603
Both means the two together.

38
00:01:52.217 --> 00:01:53.578
Both parents came.

39
00:01:54.183 --> 00:01:56.336
Your turn. Say: Both.

40
00:01:59.200 --> 00:01:59.795
Both.

41
00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:01.180
None.

42
00:02:02.183 --> 00:02:03.463
None means not any.

43
00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.144
None of them came.

44
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.089
Your turn. Say: None.

45
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:11.482
None.

46
00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:16.243
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:17.083 --> 00:02:20.105
Visal, what is the difference between each and every?

48
00:02:21.217 --> 00:02:35.133
Each focuses on individuals one by one: Each student received a certificate. Every stresses the whole group: Every student passed. Each can be used for two or more, every only for three or more.

49
00:02:36.217 --> 00:02:37.810
What about either and neither?

50
00:02:38.917 --> 00:02:49.455
They refer to two things only: Either answer is correct, and neither answer is wrong. Both means the two together. With more than two, use any, none and all.

51
00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:51.470
And whole?

52
00:02:52.583 --> 00:03:09.101
Whole means complete and comes after the article: the whole class, not all the class in this sense. With of, we say all of the class. None of them takes a singular or plural verb, singular being more formal.

53
00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:11.790
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:18.864
Grammar focus: Determiners: either and neither and both (two); each and every and all and none (more than two).

55
00:03:19.164 --> 00:03:20.467
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:21.183 --> 00:03:23.183
Each student received a certificate.

57
00:03:24.083 --> 00:03:25.758
Neither answer is correct.

58
00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.174
The whole class laughed.

59
00:03:29.133 --> 00:03:38.562
Either, neither and both = two. Each = individuals. Every = the whole group. Whole follows the article. None = not any.

60
00:03:39.317 --> 00:03:42.747
Be careful! Don't say: Every of the students came.

61
00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.118
Say: Every one of the students came.

62
00:03:47.418 --> 00:03:48.779
Use every one of.

63
00:03:49.567 --> 00:03:52.254
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:54.579
Counting Everyone In.

65
00:03:54.879 --> 00:03:57.656
Each student received a certificate for their effort.

66
00:03:58.667 --> 00:04:01.038
Every student in the school took part.

67
00:04:02.033 --> 00:04:04.590
Neither of the two teams scored in the first half.

68
00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:07.623
The whole stadium cheered at the end.

69
00:04:08.633 --> 00:04:11.271
None of the visitors left before the final whistle.

70
00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:16.287
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:19.393
blank of the two answers is correct.

72
00:04:19.693 --> 00:04:22.766
A, Neither. B, Every. C, None.

73
00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.102
The answer is A: Neither.

74
00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:32.436
blank student has a locker. (individuals)

75
00:04:32.736 --> 00:04:35.647
A, Whole. B, Each. C, Both.

76
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.292
The answer is B: Each.

77
00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:43.962
The blank class laughed.

78
00:04:44.262 --> 00:04:47.288
A, whole. B, each. C, either.

79
00:04:50.833 --> 00:04:52.415
The answer is A: whole.

80
00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.445
Both refers to blank things.

81
00:04:55.745 --> 00:04:58.760
A, many. B, two. C, three.

82
00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.091
The answer is B: two.

83
00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.818
According to the reading, who cheered at the end?

84
00:05:08.118 --> 00:05:12.259
A, the whole stadium. B, one team. C, the referee.

85
00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.125
The answer is A: the whole stadium.

86
00:05:19.333 --> 00:05:22.682
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:23.517 --> 00:05:26.410
Explain the difference between 'each' and 'every'.

88
00:05:35.217 --> 00:05:36.601
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:36.901 --> 00:05:51.177
Each looks at individuals one by one, for example each student gets a book. Every looks at the group as a whole, for example every student passed the exam. The meaning is almost the same, but the focus is different.

90
00:05:51.477 --> 00:05:53.210
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:05:54.117 --> 00:06:01.924
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and determiners: either and neither and both (two); each and every and all and none (more than two).

92
00:06:02.224 --> 00:06:07.558
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

93
00:06:07.858 --> 00:06:10.963
See you in Unit twenty-three. Goodbye!

94
00:06:11.263 --> 00:06:14.937
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

